### General Compiler Flag Remarks

Should you use ```-ffast-math``` this leads to inaccuracies in some applications,
such as ccz4. If this flag is desired, also use ```-frounding-math``` to avoid rounding
errors.
Clang enables fused multiply add operations via ```-ffp-contract=on``` instead of ```-fma```.


### OpenMP

For NVIDIA GPUs:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export MARCH=sm_XX
CC=clang CXX=clang++ ./configure --with-multithreading=omp --with-gpu=omp CXXFLAGS="-std=c++17 -O3 -W -Wall -Wno-attributes -fopenmp -fopenmp-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -Xopenmp-target=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -march=$MARCH" LDFLAGS="-march=native -mtune=native -fopenmp -fopenmp-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -Xopenmp-target=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -march=$MARCH"
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where XX has to be replaced by the actual compute capability of the device.

Note: We set the C++ compiler standard to 17. This is due to an issue with LLVM Clang itself. See https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/issues/61327.

### Using MLIR

Building a benchmark that uses MLIR requires our exahype-opt executable, which must first be built.
Navigate to src/exahype2/mlir and enter the commands
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build && cd build
cmake ..
cmake --build .
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
The output bin directory should then be added to your PATH.

### Building MLIR

MLIR is not necessarily included in an LLVM install, so it may be necessary to build from source.
First, clone llvm from GitHub.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project.git
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In general, building MLIR just involves adding "mlir" to the LLVM_ENABLE_PROJECTS CMake option.
See https://mlir.llvm.org/getting_started/ for information. However, we generally want to build with
offloading support. To do so follow the following steps. Note that it assumes the existence of some clang version,
but we will build a new version of it.

Modify the preexisting CMake cache file (llvm-project/offload/cmake/caches/Offload.cmake) to build MLIR. 
After modification, the file should start with 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
set(LLVM_ENABLE_PROJECTS "clang;clang-tools-extra;lld;mlir" CACHE STRING "")
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Finally run the following commands
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cd llvm-project
mkdir build-x86
CC=clang CXX=clang++ cmake -S llvm -B build-x86 -C $(pwd)/offload/cmake/caches/Offload.cmake \
      -DLLVM_TARGETS_TO_BUILD="host;NVPTX;AMDGPU" \
      -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DLLVM_ENABLE_ASSERTIONS=ON \
      -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$(pwd)/install-x86 \
      -G "Ninja" \
      -DLIBOMPTARGET_NVPTX_COMPUTE_CAPABILITIES="80,90" \
      -DLIBOMP_ARCH="x86_64" \
      -DOMPTARGET_ENABLE_DEBUG=ON \
      -DLIBOMPTARGET_DLOPEN_PLUGINS=""
cd build-x86
ninja
ninja install
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To use the built compiler remember to set environment variables appropriately. Here we show
the settings when using an NVIDIA GPU, where ${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY} is llvm-project/install-x86.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PATH=${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY}/bin/:$PATH
export LIBRARY_PATH=${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY}/lib/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/:$LIBRARY_PATH
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY}/lib/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
export LIBRARY_PATH=${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY}/lib/nvptx64-nvidia-cuda/:$LIBRARY_PATH
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY}/lib/nvptx64-nvidia-cuda/:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
export LIBRARY_PATH=${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY}/lib/:$LIBRARY_PATH
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=${LLVM_INSTALL_DIRECTORY}/lib/:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

On NVIDIA hardware we also require access to cuda, so appropriate variables for this too.
 */
 
